condition_name: Necrotizing Enterocolitis
condition_slug: necrotizing-enterocolitis
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "This refers to extensive necrosis of the intestine of multi-factorial origin. It may ultimately result in intestinal perforation. It commonly affects the terminal ileum and proximal colon but it may involve the entire length of the gut. Predisposing factors include: Prematurity and very low birth weight, early infant formula feeding, intra-uterine growth restriction, polycythaemia, septicaemia, umbilical catheterization, congenital heart diseases."
clinical_features[2]:
  - type: Grade 1 (Better Prognosis)
    features[6]: Feed intolerance,abdominal distension,bilious vomiting or gastric aspirate,haematochezia or melaena,"systemic illness – lethargy, hypotonia, apnea",plain abdominal X-Ray shows gaseous bowel distension or presence of gas within the bowel wall (pneumatosis intestinalis)
  - type: Grade 2 (Worse Prognosis)
    features[7]: "Grade 1, Plus:",Abdominal tenderness and rigidity (evidence of perforation),abnormal or spontaneous bleeding,shock,leucopenia,thrombocytopaenia,pneumomediastinum or portal vein gas.
investigations[5]: Full blood count,Plain abdominal X-Ray,Random Blood Glucose,"Serum electrolytes, urea and creatinine",Blood culture
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[9]: Nil per Os (NPO),Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN),Intravenous fluid therapy,Monitor abdominal girth,Insert a nasogastric tube to decompress the stomach and for regular aspiration,Serial plain abdominal X-Ray (supine and right lateral),Correct thrombocytopaenia and deranged coagulation profile with appropriate blood product,Manage shock using crystalloids or colloids and inotropes,Monitor urinary output for anti-shock therapy success
  drug[3]: "Antibiotics are administered intravenously: Cephalosporin (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or ceftazidime 100 mg/kg/day)",Gentamicin 5 mg/kg/day (or kanamycin),Metronidazole 7 mg/kg 8 hourly
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[0]:
complications[2]: Intestinal perforation,Strictures presenting with intestinal obstruction
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment[3]: Continue antibiotics and nil per os for 7 – 10 days if diagnosis is confirmed with X-Ray findings,"Re-introduce gradual oral feeds after 48 hours if diagnosis remains unconfirmed and baby recovers quickly, while antibiotic therapy continues for 5 days",Surgery is indicated by clinical deterioration or intestinal perforation
prognosis: null